From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: KmsPower <kmspower@local>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 17:05:00 
Subject: [PATCH 0107] gpu31-kms: power DECON (pd_disp) before the first register read (int65)

int64 loads the DRM modules, but the KMS probe dies on the first DECON
register read:

    PM DOMAIN: pd-disp can't add device 14830000.decon_fb
    exynos7580: probe: calling decon_kms_get()
    ...
    Kernel BUG at 0
    PC: decon_reg_get_stop_status+0xc
    LR: decon_probe_common+0x610
    x0 : ffffff8003660000   (decon->regs, mapped)

`decon_probe_common()` touches registers (the "DECON already running" VIDCON0
read) *before* its original pm_runtime_enable()/pm_runtime_get_sync() block
further down.  The vendor boot path survives this because the bootloader
leaves pd_disp and the DECON clocks on; in the KMS path the vendor probe
attempt (which refuses with -ENODEV) has already been through the driver
core's pm_request_idle, pd_disp can be off, and the read aborts.

The "pd-disp can't add device" line is the duplicate attach, not a failure:
`arch/arm64/mach-exynos/pm_domains.c` attaches on BUS_NOTIFY_BIND_DRIVER,
which `drivers/base/dd.c` sends on *every* probe attempt, and a failed probe
does not send UNBOUND, so the second (KMS) bind's __pm_genpd_add_device()
returns -EBUSY.  The domain is attached (dev->pm_domain != NULL).

Changes, all in decon_probe_common():

  - check decon_int_get_clocks()'s return (the vendor probe ignored it);
  - after the IRQ mutex init, before any register access: publish the
    platform drvdata (the runtime PM callbacks resolve decon from it),
    verify dev->pm_domain is attached (fail with -ENODEV if not), then
    pm_runtime_enable() + pm_runtime_get_sync() and log
    "decon: regs powered (pd attached), reading VIDCON0";
  - drop the later duplicate pm_runtime_enable()/pm_runtime_get_sync() pair,
    so the existing teardown's put/disable stays balanced.

--- a/drivers/video/exynos/decon_7580/decon_core.c
+++ b/drivers/video/exynos/decon_7580/decon_core.c
@@ -4702,7 +4702,12 @@
 
 	/* init clock setting for decon */
 	decon_int_drvdata = decon;
-	decon_int_get_clocks(decon);
+	ret = decon_int_get_clocks(decon);
+	if (ret) {
+		/* the vendor probe ignored this: an ERR_PTR clock aborts later */
+		decon_err("failed to get DECON clocks: %d\n", ret);
+		goto fail_kfree;
+	}
 
 	/* Get memory resource and map SFR region. */
 	res = platform_get_resource(pdev, IORESOURCE_MEM, 0);
@@ -4719,6 +4724,32 @@
 	init_waitqueue_head(&decon->wait_frmdone);
 	mutex_init(&decon->vsync_info.irq_lock);
 
+	/*
+	 * int65: DECON lives in pd_disp, and the first register access in this
+	 * function (the "already running" VIDCON0 read) happens before the
+	 * original pm_runtime_enable()/get_sync().  On the vendor boot path the
+	 * bootloader leaves the domain and the clocks on, so that read works;
+	 * in the KMS path the vendor probe attempt has already gone through
+	 * pm_request_idle, pd_disp can be OFF, and readl aborts.  The platform
+	 * bus BIND_DRIVER notifier has attached the domain by now, so bring it
+	 * (and the clocks) up here, before touching any register, and fail
+	 * cleanly if it cannot be powered.
+	 */
+	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, decon);
+	if (!dev->pm_domain) {
+		decon_err("DECON has no power domain attached (pd_disp)\n");
+		ret = -ENODEV;
+		goto fail_irq_mutex;
+	}
+	pm_runtime_enable(dev);
+	ret = pm_runtime_get_sync(dev);
+	if (ret < 0) {
+		decon_err("failed to power DECON power domain: %d\n", ret);
+		pm_runtime_disable(dev);
+		goto fail_irq_mutex;
+	}
+	dev_info(dev, "decon: regs powered (pd attached), reading VIDCON0\n");
+
 	snprintf(device_name, MAX_NAME_SIZE, "decon%d", DECON_INT);
 	decon->timeline = sw_sync_timeline_create(device_name);
 	decon->timeline_max = 1;
@@ -4906,19 +4937,12 @@
 		decon_err("failed to set lcd information\n");
 		goto fail_thread;
 	}
-	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, decon);
-	pm_runtime_enable(dev);
-
-
 	decon->max_win_bw = decon_calc_bandwidth(decon->lcd_info->xres,
 			decon->lcd_info->yres, 4,
 			decon->lcd_info->fps);
 
-#if defined(CONFIG_PM_RUNTIME)
-	pm_runtime_get_sync(decon->dev);
-#else
-	decon_runtime_resume(decon->dev);
-#endif
+	/* int65: pm_runtime was enabled and the device powered above, before
+	 * the first register access. */
 
 	/* gpu31-kms: register_fb == false is the KMS stand-up path.  The DRM
 	 * driver owns the pipe and programs DECON itself, and no fb0 exists
